    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let us examine the conversation line by line.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Доброе утро, Виктор.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Good morning, Viktor.' Russian leaves out 'am', 'is', or 'are' in the present tense, linking 'good' straight to 'Viktor'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Доброе утро.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Good morning.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'good' with 'morning'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Виктор приготовил такой омлет.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Victor prepared such an omelet.' — Notice the proper name meaning 'Viktor' paired with the verb for 'cooked'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Это типа извинения?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Is this a kind of apology?' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'this' with 'apologies'. Then Chef Viktor responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Это типа омлет.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'This is a kind of omelet.' Russian omits the present-tense verb 'to be', connecting 'this' directly with 'omelette'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ешь давай.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Come on, eat.' In Russian, 'eat' leads into 'let's' in a very natural everyday phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я опоздаю на работу.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I will be late for work.' Notice how 'I' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'job'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Да, кушайте быстрее.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yes, eat faster.' The character combines 'yes' with 'faster' in a natural Russian turn of phrase. Then Lyova responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Ладно, я съем это.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Alright, I will eat this.' Lyova links 'all right' directly with 'I' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Это очень вкусно.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'This is very delicious.' A compact expression built around 'this' and 'it's tasty'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Нам пора идти.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'It is time for us to go.' Listen for the word meaning 'us' right alongside 'it's time' in this line. Lyova answers straight away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Спасибо за завтрак.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
